  "vendor": "Novation",
  "source": "Novation Launch Control XL 3 appendix, https://userguides.novationmusic.com/hc/en-gb/articles/26260540230162-Launch-Control-XL-3-appendix, section 'Default Mode (16) parameters' (Mode 16, the fixed default mode; all controls on MIDI channel 16: encoders CC13-36, faders CC5-12, button rows CC37-44 and CC45-52). The encoders' relative/absolute encoding is not stated there, so abs7 is used; use Auto-detect in the inspector if yours sends relative values. FRO339 (docs/control/midi-remote-device-handshake.md#device-handshake): Play and Record now work, per the Launch Control XL 3 Programmer's Reference Guide, https://fael-downloads-prod.focusrite.com/customer/prod/downloads/launch_control_xl_3_programmer_s_reference_guide-pdf_en.pdf -- p.7 'standalone (MIDI) mode' says Page/Track/Record/Play/Solo-Arm/Mute-Select send nothing there; p.8 'DAW mode control' says sending SysEx F0 00 20 29 02 15 02 7F F7 puts the device into DAW mode (F0 00 20 29 02 15 02 00 F7 reverts it), matching Ableton Live 12's own script (hello on connect, goodbye on unload/quit); p.9 says DAW mode then reports Play (CC116) and Record (CC118) on channel 1, with encoders/faders unchanged (channel 16, same CC13-36/5-12 numbers) -- over the DAW In/Out (USB) interface, a SEPARATE port from the plain MIDI In/Out Custom Modes normally use (p.5, p.8). Confirmed on hardware 2026-09-28 (a connected Launch Control XL 3): in DAW mode, Play/Record/encoders/faders all arrive on the 'DAW Out' source with exactly these CC numbers, nothing on 'MIDI Out' -- so this template's handshake AND every one of its controls (including Play/Record) must be set up on ONE input, the device's 'DAW' port (e.g. 'LCXL3 1 DAW Out'), never its 'MIDI' port; the Add-controller popover preselects it automatically for this template via this document's own 'handshake.port' hint. The same test also found CoreMIDI naming this device's four ports asymmetrically -- an input named '...DAW Out' pairs with an OUTPUT named '...DAW In', never a device sharing that name -- which is why the handshake itself is resolved by ControllerHandshake.h's resolveHandshakeOutput() rather than a literal name match.",
